{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_067f8848735d9d840169bb165276748191a3443c54a68770ec", "created": 1773868626, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**IMAGING TESTS** are a broad category of medical diagnostic procedures that create pictures of internal body structures to help clinicians detect, locate, and characterize disease. IMAGING TESTS are used across many cancer and hematologic evaluations—including but not limited to AML and ALL, lymphoma (e.g., follicular lymphoma), and solid tumors such as breast, ovarian, bladder, prostate, kidney, stomach, rectal, mouth, throat, nasopharyngeal (NPC), adrenal, bone, and cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC). They are commonly performed during initial workup and pre-treatment staging, and may also be ordered during surveillance or when symptoms arise or recurrence/relapse is suspected.\n\nIMAGING TESTS help identify the primary tumor site (where cancer started), assess tumor size, location, and features, and determine whether disease has spread to nearby tissues, organs, or lymph nodes (regional spread) or to distant body sites (metastasis). In lymphomas and leukemias, IMAGING TESTS can help evaluate disease involvement beyond primary sites (including outside the bone marrow for leukemia) and can also be used to identify complications that may affect care, such as infection or bleeding. For some cancers (e.g., breast), IMAGING TESTS can visualize suspicious lymph nodes and guide decisions about whether biopsy or lymph node sampling is needed. In ovarian cancer, IMAGING TESTS also contribute to assessing disease extent and the feasibility of surgery.\n\nCommon examples of IMAGING TESTS include **CT** (including targeted studies such as brain CT or chest CT), **MRI** (including brain MRI), **ultrasound**, **PET** (including **FDG-PET** and combined **PET/CT**), **mammography**, **chest x-ray**, and other modalities such as **bone scans**; specialized procedures such as **endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)** may also be used in certain settings (e.g., rectal cancer). Some IMAGING TESTS may require preparation (such as fasting or stopping certain medications) and may use **contrast material** to improve image clarity, while others do not.\n\nResults from IMAGING TESTS are typically **interpreted by a radiologist**, documented in a formal report, and communicated to the treating clinician and discussed with the patient. Imaging findings are frequently combined with other diagnostic information—especially **biopsy**, and in blood cancers **blood and bone marrow tests** (and sometimes biomarkers)—to determine disease status, assign clinical stage and TNM scores when applicable, and inform individualized treatment planning and monitoring for response or recurrence.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 527, "prompt_tokens": 1662, "total_tokens": 2189, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}